<title>NETHERLANDS: Amsterdam-Rotterdam-Antwerp stock levels rise.</title>
<headline>Amsterdam-Rotterdam-Antwerp stock levels rise.</headline>
<dateline>AMSTERDAM 1996-09-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Oil product inventories held in independent tankage in the Amsterdam-Rotterdam-Antwerp area were at the following levels, with week-ago and year-ago levels, industry sources said.</p>
<p>All figures in thousands of tonnes:</p>
<p>			12/9/96		   5/9/96	     15/9/95</p>
<p>    Gasoline	450-475		  425-450		   425</p>
<p>    Naphtha		50			 75		    75</p>
<p>    Gas oil   1,550-1,600		    1,600	 2,050-2,100</p>
<p>    Fuel oil	300-325			350		   450</p>
<p>    Jet kerosene	 20			 20		    30</p>
<p>Motor gasoline stocks rose with rapid movements in and out of barges and cargoes, some of the latter going transatlantic.</p>
<p>Naphtha inventories dipped slightly as the only movement involved barges leaving for the German chemical industry.</p>
<p>Gas oil stocks dipped as cargoes came from the former Soviet Union and some product was stored in readiness for IPE delivery, but more left as heating oil to the traditional markets.</p>
<p>Fuel oil inventories fell as few cargoes arrived, but product left to the bunker sector and to Germany.</p>
<p>Jet fuel stocks changed little with slight aviation interest.</p>
<p>-- Philip Blenkinsop, Amsterdam Newsroom, 31 20 504 5000</p>
</text>
